---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FersCalculator from "../components/FersCalculator.astro";
import RetirementTypeChecker from "../components/RetirementTypeChecker.astro";

const title = "FERS Retirement Calculator: Estimate Your Federal Pension";
const description =
  "Free FERS retirement calculator for federal employees. Estimate your pension, FERS Supplement, and survivor benefit, then compare retirement dates.";
const lastReviewed = "2026-08-14";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How is the FERS pension calculated?",
    answer:
      "The basic FERS annuity uses High-3 Average Salary, years of creditable service, and a multiplier. The standard multiplier is 1% per year. If you retire at age 62 or later with at least 20 years of service, the multiplier rises to 1.1%. Special category employees such as law enforcement officers, firefighters, and air traffic controllers use 1.7% for the first 20 years and 1% thereafter.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the High-3 Average Salary?",
    answer:
      "High-3 is the highest average basic pay you earned during any 36 consecutive months of federal service. For most employees, this is the final three years before retirement. It includes basic pay and locality pay, but excludes overtime, bonuses, and most premium pay. Check OPM guidance for the exact pay types included in your situation.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does unused sick leave increase my FERS pension?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Unused sick leave is converted to creditable service when your annuity is computed. OPM uses a factor of 2,087 hours per year. Sick leave credit can increase the service figure used in the pension formula, but it does not make you eligible to retire earlier.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the FERS MRA+10 retirement?",
    answer:
      "MRA+10 allows you to retire at your Minimum Retirement Age with at least 10 years of service, but your annuity may be reduced if you begin benefits before age 62. The reduction is 5% per year under age 62, applied by full months as 5/12 of 1% per month. The reduction is avoided with 30 years of service, or with 20 years of service when benefits begin at age 60.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the FERS Special Retirement Supplement?",
    answer:
      "The FERS Supplement is an extra payment that may be paid to eligible federal employees who retire before age 62. It is not Social Security and stops at age 62. This calculator uses an approximate method: your estimated Social Security benefit at 62 multiplied by your FERS service years divided by 40. The result is only an estimate and can be reduced by earned income in some cases.",
  },
  {
    question: "How does the survivor benefit reduce my FERS annuity?",
    answer:
      "A full survivor benefit usually reduces your annuity by 10% and provides your spouse with 50% of your unreduced annuity after your death. A partial survivor benefit usually reduces your annuity by 5% and provides 25%. Choosing no survivor benefit leaves your own annuity unreduced but provides no spouse benefit.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does this calculator account for military service buyback?",
    answer:
      "Yes. The advanced Military Buyback Years field is meant for military time for which you have paid a deposit. Do not enter military time that has not been credited through the buyback process.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the difference between deferred and postponed retirement?",
    answer:
      "A deferred retirement is for employees who leave federal service before qualifying for an immediate annuity and collect later. A postponed retirement is for employees who already qualify for an immediate annuity but delay beginning it, often to reduce the MRA+10 age penalty. Postponed retirement may also allow FEHB coverage to resume later, while deferred retirement generally does not.",
  },
  {
    question: "Does this calculator work for CSRS or CSRS-Offset employees?",
    answer:
      "No, not reliably. This calculator uses FERS formulas. CSRS and CSRS-Offset have different multipliers and eligibility rules. If you are in one of those systems, use the system selector for a warning and verify your estimate with OPM or your HR office.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I include Social Security and TSP when planning my FERS retirement?",
    answer:
      "Yes. FERS is a predictable pension, but most federal employees should view it alongside Social Security and TSP. This calculator now includes a projected monthly income section that combines FERS, Supplement, Social Security, TSP, and other income.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the FERS COLA?",
    answer:
      "FERS cost-of-living adjustments help protect the purchasing power of your annuity, but eligibility and timing depend on your retirement type. This page explains COLA at a high level. For a formal calculation, check OPM guidance and your retirement records.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is included in my High-3 salary?",
    answer:
      "High-3 generally includes basic pay and locality pay. It excludes overtime, bonuses, cash awards, travel reimbursements, and most premium pay. OPM looks at every possible 36 consecutive months of service and uses the highest average.",
  },
  {
    question: "How much service credit does my unused sick leave provide?",
    answer:
      "Unused sick leave is converted using a factor of 2,087 hours per year. For example, 520 hours is about 3 months, 1,040 hours is about 6 months, and 2,087 hours is about 1 year. Sick leave credit increases the pension calculation but does not make you eligible to retire earlier.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are the survivor benefit choices for FERS?",
    answer:
      "No survivor benefit leaves your annuity unreduced but provides no spouse benefit. Partial survivor benefit usually reduces your annuity by 5% and provides 25% of the unreduced annuity. Full survivor benefit usually reduces your annuity by 10% and provides 50%.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can the FERS Supplement be reduced by earned income?",
    answer:
      "Yes. The FERS Special Retirement Supplement is subject to an earnings test before age 62. If your earned income exceeds the annual limit, the Supplement can be reduced or eliminated. This calculator estimates the Supplement but does not apply the earnings test.",
  },
  {
    question: "When should I start the federal retirement application process?",
    answer:
      "Start planning 12 to 18 months before retirement. Verify service, High-3, leave balances, and retirement system with HR 6 to 12 months out. Prepare forms and beneficiary choices around 90 days before retirement, then complete the final review before your official retirement date.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is this estimate my official FERS benefit?",
    answer:
      "No. This is an educational estimate. Your official annuity is determined by OPM and your agency using your official personnel and pay records. Before making a retirement decision, verify your service computation date, leave balances, and retirement system with your HR office.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I retire at age 60 with 20 years of FERS service?",
    answer:
      "Yes, 20 years of creditable service at age 60 is an immediate FERS retirement age and service combination. It also avoids the MRA+10 age reduction that can apply to earlier retirement. If you retire at 62 or later with at least 20 years of service, the standard multiplier rises from 1.0% to 1.1%.",
  },
  {
    question: "What happens to my unused annual leave when I retire?",
    answer:
      "Unused annual leave is typically paid out as a lump sum, subject to agency limits and current law. Annual leave is not converted to creditable service in the same way as unused sick leave. Check with HR before retirement to plan the timing of your departure.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I start a military service buyback?",
    answer:
      "Contact your agency HR or benefits office to request a military deposit estimate. You generally need your DD-214 and the dates of active military service. Paying the deposit makes that military time creditable for FERS eligibility and annuity calculation. Do not enter unpaid military time in the calculator.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is the difference between the FERS Supplement and Social Security?",
    answer:
      "The FERS Special Retirement Supplement is paid to certain eligible FERS retirees before age 62. It is not Social Security and ends at age 62. Social Security is a separate benefit you may claim separately, generally from age 62 or later. The calculator estimates the Supplement but does not estimate your Social Security claiming strategy.",
  },
  {
    question: "What documents should I gather before applying for federal retirement?",
    answer:
      "Start with your service computation date, personnel actions, recent pay and leave statements, military service records or DD-214 if military buyback applies, beneficiary forms, TSP statements, and any prior federal service records. HR and OPM may also need marriage and dependent documents if you elect a survivor benefit.",
  },
  {
    question: "How often should I review my FERS estimate?",
    answer:
      "Review it whenever your High-3 salary, service credit, retirement system, leave balance, or planned retirement date changes. At minimum, update it annually and again 12 to 18 months before retirement, when you should begin verifying official records with HR.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "WebPage",
      "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#webpage",
      url: "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/",
      name: title,
      description,
      inLanguage: "en",
      datePublished: lastReviewed,
      dateModified: lastReviewed,
      mainEntity: {
        "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#app",
      },
      about: {
        "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#app",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "FERS Retirement Calculator",
          item: "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#calculator",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "WebApplication",
      "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#app",
      name: "FERS Retirement Calculator",
      url: "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/",
      description,
      inLanguage: "en",
      applicationCategory: "FinanceApplication",
      operatingSystem: "Web",
      browserRequirements: "Requires JavaScript",
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      offers: {
        "@type": "Offer",
        price: "0",
        priceCurrency: "USD",
      },
      featureList: [
        "FERS pension estimate",
        "High-3 salary input",
        "Creditable service calculation",
        "Unused sick leave conversion",
        "Military buyback service credit",
        "Special category multiplier",
        "Survivor benefit reduction",
        "Survivor benefit comparison",
        "FERS Supplement estimate",
        "MRA+10 comparison",
        "Retirement age scenario switching",
        "Shareable estimate link",
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "TechArticle",
      "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#article",
      headline: "How the FERS Retirement Formula Works",
      description,
      articleSection: [
        "FERS formula",
        "High-3 salary",
        "Creditable service",
        "FERS Supplement",
        "Survivor benefit",
        "Deferred retirement",
        "Postponed retirement",
        "FERS COLA",
      ],
      inLanguage: "en",
      datePublished: lastReviewed,
      dateModified: lastReviewed,
      author: {
        "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#organization",
      },
      publisher: {
        "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#organization",
      },
      about: {
        "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#app",
      },
      mainEntityOfPage: {
        "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#webpage",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      inLanguage: "en",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        name: faq.question,
        inLanguage: "en",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: faq.answer,
          inLanguage: "en",
        },
      })),
    },
    {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "@id": "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/#organization",
      name: "FERS Retirement Calculator",
      url: "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/",
      logo: "https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/favicon.svg",
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://fers-retirement-calculator.com/"
  schema={schema}
  updatedTime={lastReviewed}
>
  <section class="pb-12 pt-10 sm:pb-16 sm:pt-14">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-normal text-accent">
          Federal retirement planning
        </p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-normal text-ink sm:text-5xl">
          FERS Retirement Calculator
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-base leading-relaxed text-muted sm:text-lg">
          Estimate your FERS pension, compare retirement dates, and understand the rule behind every number. Free, private, and no sign-up required.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-5">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Quick answer</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The FERS basic annuity is
            <span class="font-mono font-semibold text-ink">
              High-3 × Years of Creditable Service × Multiplier
            </span>.
            This calculator also includes unused sick leave, military buyback, special category rules, survivor benefits, FERS Supplement, and a comparison of retiring at MRA+10, 60, or 62.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8">
        <FersCalculator lastReviewed={lastReviewed} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works" class="border-t border-line bg-surface">
    <div class="page-shell section-pad">
      <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.7fr_1.3fr] lg:gap-16">
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-normal text-accent">How it works</p>
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-normal text-ink sm:text-4xl">
            Three numbers give you a starting point.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The basic FERS formula is simple. The detail is in what counts as salary, service, and the right multiplier.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-canvas/50 p-6">
            <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-accent">01</p>
            <h3 class="mt-4 text-base font-semibold text-ink">High-3 salary</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
              The highest average basic pay from any 36 consecutive months of federal service.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-canvas/50 p-6">
            <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-accent">02</p>
            <h3 class="mt-4 text-base font-semibold text-ink">Creditable service</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
              FERS-covered years, unused sick leave, and military time after buyback.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-canvas/50 p-6">
            <p class="text-lg font-semibold text-accent">03</p>
            <h3 class="mt-4 text-base font-semibold text-ink">Multiplier</h3>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
              1%, 1.1%, or special category rates depending on age, service, and position.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line bg-canvas/50 p-6">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">How the MRA+10 reduction works</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-sm font-semibold leading-7 text-accent">
          Reduction = 5% x full years under age 62 + (5% / 12) x additional months
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Example: a 57-year-old with 25 years of service begins benefits at MRA. The reduction is 5% x 5 years = 25%. If the unreduced annuity is $25,000, the reduced annual annuity is $18,750.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The reduction is avoided with at least 30 years of service, or with at least 20 years of service when benefits begin at age 60.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="formula" class="section-pad">
    <div class="page-shell grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] lg:gap-16">
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-normal text-accent">FERS formula</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-normal text-ink sm:text-4xl">
          One formula, three multipliers.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The basic FERS annuity is High-3 Average Salary multiplied by creditable service multiplied by the correct multiplier.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold leading-7 text-ink">Annual Pension = High-3 x Service x Multiplier</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Example: a $100,000 High-3 with 25 years of service at the 1.1% multiplier gives $27,500 per year.
          </p>
        </div>
        <img
          src="/fers-annuity-formula.svg"
          alt="FERS annuity formula: High-3 average salary times creditable service times multiplier equals annual FERS pension."
          width="720"
          height="200"
          class="mt-6 h-auto w-full max-w-xl rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[640px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-canvas text-muted">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Situation</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Multiplier</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">When it applies</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">Standard FERS</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-accent">1.0%</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">Most employees who retire before age 62, or at 62+ with less than 20 years.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">Enhanced</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-accent">1.1%</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">Age 62 or later with at least 20 years of service.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">Special category</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-accent">1.7% / 1.0%</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">First 20 years at 1.7%, then 1% for LEO, ATC, firefighters, and nuclear materials couriers.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:col-span-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="flex h-full flex-col rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-accent-soft text-accent">
              <i class="ph ph-calculator text-lg" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </span>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Standard example</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-accent">$90,000 x 22 x 1.0%</p>
          <p class="mt-3 flex-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            A 58-year-old with 22 years of service receives $19,800 per year before any MRA+10 reduction.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-5 flex items-center justify-between rounded-lg bg-canvas/70 px-3 py-2 text-sm">
            <span class="text-muted">Annual pension</span>
            <span class="font-semibold text-ink">$19,800</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex h-full flex-col rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-accent-soft text-accent">
              <i class="ph ph-trend-up text-lg" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </span>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Enhanced example</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-accent">$110,000 x 24 x 1.1%</p>
          <p class="mt-3 flex-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            At age 62 with 24 years of service, the 1.1% multiplier gives $29,040 per year.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-5 flex items-center justify-between rounded-lg bg-canvas/70 px-3 py-2 text-sm">
            <span class="text-muted">Annual pension</span>
            <span class="font-semibold text-ink">$29,040</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex h-full flex-col rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="inline-flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-accent-soft text-accent">
              <i class="ph ph-shield-star text-lg" aria-hidden="true"></i>
            </span>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Special category example</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-accent">$95,000 x 20 x 1.7%</p>
          <p class="mt-3 flex-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            For the first 20 years, a law enforcement officer receives $32,300 per year before applying the regular 1% multiplier to additional service.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-5 flex items-center justify-between rounded-lg bg-canvas/70 px-3 py-2 text-sm">
            <span class="text-muted">Annual pension</span>
            <span class="font-semibold text-ink">$32,300</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="mra-10" class="border-t border-line bg-surface">
    <div class="page-shell section-pad">
      <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] lg:gap-16">
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-normal text-accent">MRA+10</p>
          <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-normal text-ink sm:text-4xl">
            Early retirement has a cost.
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
            You can retire at MRA with 10 years of service, but beginning benefits before 62 can reduce your annuity by 5% per year.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-line bg-canvas/50 p-6">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">No reduction when:</p>
            <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
              <li>You have at least 30 years of service.</li>
              <li>You have at least 20 years of service and begin at age 60.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[560px] text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-canvas text-muted">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Birth year</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Minimum Retirement Age</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-line">
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1970 or later</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">57</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1969</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">56 years 10 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1965 to 1968</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">56 years 2 to 8 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1965</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">56 years 2 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1966</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">56 years 4 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1967</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">56 years 6 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1968</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">56 years 8 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1953 to 1964</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">56</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1948 to 1952</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">55 years 2 to 10 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1952</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">55 years 10 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1951</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">55 years 8 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1950</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 text-muted">55 years 6 months</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-4 font-semibold text-ink">1949</td>
