  <section id="high-3-details" class="section-pad">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <div class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-normal text-accent">High-3 explained</p>
        <h2 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-normal text-ink sm:text-4xl">
          What actually goes into your High-3
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-base leading-relaxed text-muted">
          High-3 is the highest average basic pay you earn during any 36 consecutive months of federal service. It is not necessarily your final salary, and it is not your total compensation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Generally included</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            <li>Base salary</li>
            <li>Locality pay</li>
            <li>Basic pay earned during the qualifying 36-month period</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Generally excluded</h3>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            <li>Overtime</li>
            <li>Bonuses</li>
            <li>Cash awards</li>
            <li>Travel reimbursements</li>
            <li>Most premium pay</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">How to think about the 36-month window</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          OPM looks at every possible consecutive 36-month period in your career and uses the highest average. That means an earlier period could be higher than your final years if you took a lower-paying position late in your career. A promotion or step increase inside the highest window can lift the average and increase your pension for life.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-line bg-canvas/50 p-6">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Step-by-step example</h3>
        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Year 1</p>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-accent">$95,000</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Year 2</p>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-accent">$100,000</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface p-4">
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">Year 3</p>
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-accent">$98,000</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The three-year average is ($95,000 + $100,000 + $98,000) / 3 = $97,667. This becomes the High-3 salary used in the annuity formula.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

